<v Orlando>It may work, it may not.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:55.250
<v Orlando>Let's go back and read our dialogue one more time.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.550
<v Orlando>And so Michelle, you're going to start us off, right?

00:11:58.600 --> 00:11:59.100
<v Michelle>Okay.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:06.000
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Não sei, tô me sentindo meio gorda ... se a gente</lang>
<lang pt-BR>for jantar hoje à noite eu só vou tomar água.</lang>

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:13.620
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Eu vou tomar refrigerante e o quanto eu puder, afinal com</lang>
<lang pt-BR>esse sistema de refil a gente pode beber o quanto quiser.</lang>

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:17.800
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Eh, mas quando nós chegarmos lá fique atento com os copos...</lang>

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:22.393
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Fique tranqüila, os copos são</lang>
<lang pt-BR>diferentes. E se estivermos com muita</lang>

00:12:22.393 --> 00:12:26.800
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>fome, será que eles vão deixar a gente repetir sem pagar de novo?</lang>

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.790
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Claro que não! O refil é só pra bebida.</lang>

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:34.340
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Se Deus quiser a gente ainda vai adotar esse sistema no Brasil.</lang>

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:35.440
<v Valdo>There you go.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:38.300
<v Orlando><lang pt-BR>Se Deus quiser</lang>. Everybody has to learn that phrase.

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:40.300
<v Valdo>It's very, very useful.

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:46.062
<v Orlando>One final cultural note: I thought it was interesting you
talked about taking care of the cup that you're using.

00:12:46.062 --> 00:12:48.000
<v Orlando>Why did you add that to your dialogue?

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:50.800
<v Orlando>What glass you're using?

00:12:50.850 --> 00:13:02.698
<v Valdo>Because, like, in the places that, like, over here, like, if
you buy things and you order soda, they're going to give, like,

00:13:02.698 --> 00:13:04.800
<v Valdo>a color cup, you know?

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:06.050
<v Orlando>Oh, I know.

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:07.050
<v Michelle>Yeah, yeah.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:08.800
<v Orlando>If you order water, they give you…

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.000
<v Valdo>One type of cup. Yeah, it's going to be a different color.

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:14.580
<v Michelle>And so you can't go and put that in the
soda machine and get out your Coke, okay?

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:18.460
<v Valdo>So you cannot cheat. Basically, that's
the idea. They try to control it.

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:20.400
<v Orlando>But in Brazil it doesn't work in that way?

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:23.300
<v Valdo>It wouldn't, I don't think so.

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:25.600
<v Valdo>But they give you the cups filled.

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:26.300
<v Michelle>Filled, yeah.

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:31.070
<v Orlando>Well, everybody head to our website of <lang pt-BR>Tá</lang>
<lang pt-BR>Falado</lang>, check out the PDF files, join the blog,

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:35.790
<v Orlando>be able to see these words so they kind of
make sense and stick in your brain a bit more.

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:37.790
<v Orlando>And we'll come back soon with another lesson. That's <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>.

